course: dictionary
lesson: 5182
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists each meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «An itchy rash after a new medicine can be an allergic reaction.» Here itchy is about a feeling on the skin that makes you want to scratch, because a rash is a skin problem.
explain: Look at the words around the hard word. «Poison ivy gives an itchy, blistering rash» tells you itchy is about skin again, while «Maya's new jumper is itchy» means the jumper itself makes her skin want to scratch. The word it describes changes what it means to the reader.
explain: Sometimes the meaning is not about skin at all. «I have itchy feet this summer» does not mean your feet are scratchy. Itchy feet means you want to travel or move on. Ask yourself: does this make sense if I picture an itch? If not, the context is pointing to a different meaning.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Many words have more than one meaning, so check which one fits the sentence.
fact: «Itchy» means you have a feeling on your skin that makes you want to scratch.
fact: «Itchy» can also describe something that makes your skin itch, such as «an itchy jumper».
fact: «Itchy feet» means wanting to travel or move on. Scratching scratches are not involved.
exercise: «An itchy rash after a new medicine can be an allergic reaction.» What does itchy mean here? | Very loud and noisy | Very cold to touch | * A feeling on the skin that makes you want to scratch | Wanting to travel far away
exercise: Maya says, «My new wool jumper is itchy.» What is the jumper doing? | Making her laugh | Making her feel sleepy | * Making her skin want to scratch | Making her want to travel
exercise: Dad says, «I have itchy feet this summer.» What does he mean? | His feet are cold and wet | His feet are covered in bites | Hay fever is making his eyes sore | * He wants to travel or move on
test: «Hay fever brings sneezing and itchy eyes.» Which explanation fits itchy here? | Wanting to travel | Sounding very loud | * Feeling a need to rub or scratch | Feeling very tired
test: What clue in «Poison ivy gives an itchy, blistering rash» tells you itchy is about skin? | The word North | The word leaves | * The word rash | The word ivy
exam: Jo reads «itchy feet» and thinks her feet are sore. What is the best way to check her idea? | Guess quickly and move on without checking | Skip the phrase and never think about it again | Decide it means wet, because feet can be wet | * Swap in wanting to travel and see if the sentence still makes sense
